<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">N. F. S. Grundtvig</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1783">1783</a>–<a href="/years/1872">1872</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Nikolaj Frederik Severin Grundtvig</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">N. F. S. Grundtvig was a Danish pastor, poet, historian and educator. He wrote some 1,500 hymns and promoted a joyful, popular <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a> and Danish national culture. His ideas inspired the folk high schools, adult education colleges for young rural people founded from 1844, which, together with the cooperative movement, transformed the Danish countryside.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/n-f-s-grundtvig.jpg" alt="N. F. S. Grundtvig" width="320" height="415" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Christian Albrecht Jensen · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:N-f-s-grundtvig-portr%C3%A6t.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Grundtvig was born at Udby, the son of the Lutheran pastor Johan Ottosen Grundtvig, and was called Frederik by those close to him. His mother respected old Norse legends and traditions, and he was schooled in the European Enlightenment but also influenced by German romanticism and the ancient history of the Nordic countries. At nine he was sent to the pastor Laurids Svindt Feld at Thyregod, then attended the cathedral school of Aarhus from 1798. He entered the University of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/copenhagen">Copenhagen</a> in 1801, and near the end of his studies began to read Icelandic and the sagas.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>After working as a tutor on Langeland from 1805, he returned to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/copenhagen">Copenhagen</a> in 1808 and had success with Northern Mythology and a long drama in 1809. His first sermon, in 1810, denounced the city's clergy and offended the church authorities. In 1810 he went through a religious crisis and converted to a strongly held <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lutheranism">Lutheranism</a>, retiring to his father's parish at Udby as chaplain. His rate of publication in the following years was staggering, including two more world histories and, from 1816 to 1819, the journal Danne-Virke, which he almost wrote alone.</p><p>His 1825 pamphlet against the theologian Henrik Nicolai Clausen led to a conviction for libel, a fine and a seven-year ban on preaching. During it he visited <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> three times between 1829 and 1831 and studied <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anglo-saxons">Anglo-Saxon</a>.</p><p>In 1839 he became pastor of the Vartov hospital church in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/copenhagen">Copenhagen</a>, which he held until his death. From 1844 he took part in politics, and in 1848 he sat in the Constituent Assembly that wrote <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/denmark">Denmark</a>'s first constitution. He received the titular rank of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishop</a> in 1861, without a see. He wrote or translated about 1,500 hymns, and his hymn book replaced the slow measures of orthodox <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lutheranism">Lutheranism</a> with the national poets' hymns.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Works and ideas</h2><p>Grundtvig called himself a pastor, not a theologian. His theology put the authority of the living word in place of the apostolic commentaries, and he wished each congregation to be a practically independent community. He coined the name Asatro for the pre-Christian Scandinavian faith, known until then as the old customs.</p><p>He is regarded as the ideological father of the folk high school, though his own plan was to reform Sorø Academy into a popular school teaching practical skills as well as national poetry and history. The plan nearly succeeded under Christian VIII but ended with the king's death in 1848. One of his followers, Kristen Kold, had already founded the first folk high school. He opposed compulsion in education, including examinations. In 1820 he published Bjovulfs Drape, the first full translation of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beowulf">Beowulf</a> into a modern language.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “N. F. S. Grundtvig” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Cooperative dairies and the folk high schools inspired by N.F.S. <b>Grundtvig</b> later transformed rural life, and the twentieth century built a tax-funded welfare state.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-denmark">Kingdom of Denmark · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>Sharon Turner translated selected verses into modern English in 1805, <b>N. F. S. Grundtvig</b> made the first complete verse translation, in Danish, in 1820, and nine complete English translations appeared in the 19th century, among them John Mitchell Kemble's and that of William Morris and A. J. Wyatt.</blockquote><a href="/work/beowulf">Beowulf · Legacy</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Udby, in what is now <a href="/countries/denmark">Denmark</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/copenhagen">Copenhagen</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/denmark">Denmark</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=55.08,11.95&amp;year=1840" aria-label="Udby on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4812 281 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of N. F. S. Grundtvig"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5262 551h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5262 551h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=55.08,11.95&amp;year=1840">What was here in 1840? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
